---
description: Git commit and pull request conventions
---

## Git commits

- Use imperative mood and sentence case (e.g. `Add user billing flags`, `Fix sidebar layout on mobile`).
- Do **not** use Conventional Commits prefixes (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`). The history uses plain subject lines.
- Do **not** put issue-tracker identifiers in commit messages. Link the work from the pull request description instead.
- Keep the first line under ~72 characters; use the body for extra detail if needed.

## Pull requests

- Write the PR **title** and **description** in **English**.
- Do **not** put issue identifiers or tracking links in the PR **title**. Reference them inline in the description if needed.
- **Title:** same tone as commits — imperative mood, sentence case, short (under ~72 characters), no Conventional-Commits prefixes.
- **Description** — use only these two sections:
  - **Summary** — what changed for maintainers and any user-visible behavior (2–4 sentences).
  - **Changes** — bullet list by area (e.g. API routes, services, UI routes).
- Run **`pnpm check`** from the repository root before every push, including a push to an open pull request. Fix failures first.
